The legal texts come from Bahamas Laws On-line, the Government of The Bahamas' publication of the Statute Law of
The Bahamas:

  Bahamas Laws On-line, https://laws.bahamas.gov.bs (consolidated Acts as PDFs, the act index and the Acts by Year
  lists, retrieved 2026-10-29).

The publisher's notice (https://laws.bahamas.gov.bs/cms/?view=article&id=115:copyright&catid=14:general-articles):

  "The legislation contained on this website is subject to Government of The Bahamas Copyright protection. Any
  reproduction shall ensure its accuracy, and the source and copyright status of the material is made evident to
  users. The website may only be used for informational non-commercial purposes and must not be used in a derogatory
  or defamatory manner."

The site's footer reads "(c) 2026 The Government of The Bahamas - All Rights Reserved". Its page metadata carries a
different rights line naming the Isle of Man Attorney General's Chambers; that line comes from the iLAWSSYNC software
the site runs on and is not the Bahamas notice.

No licence to republish is given. This repository is therefore private; whether and how it may be published is the
owner's decision.

Changes made by Lexifina: each consolidation was converted from its PDF text layer to one Markdown file per section,
article or schedule, one paragraph per line; spacing, quotes and dashes were normalised and end-of-line hyphens
joined; running heads, footnotes, marginal notes, and Part, Book and Title headings were left out; and the versions
were arranged as git commits credited to the laws the publisher names for each change. The wording of the law was not
changed. The Government of The Bahamas does not endorse this repository.

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the tools) are
dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode
